.TH EDID-EDITOR-CLI 1 2026-09-15 "EDID Editor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
edid\-editor\-cli \- read and change EDID data from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B edid\-editor\-cli
.I command
.RI [ option ...]
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B edid\-editor\-cli
reads and changes EDID, the data a display sends to describe itself, with the same
parser as
.BR edid\-editor (1),
and without a display.
It reads the EDID base block, CTA-861 extensions and DisplayID 1.x and 2.x blocks.
.PP
A
.I file
is binary EDID or hexadecimal text in the layouts
.BR edid\-decode (1)
and
.B xrandr \-\-verbose
print; the kind is recognised from the data, and
.B \-
reads standard input.
The EDID of a connected display is read from its
.I edid
file under
.IR /sys/class/drm ;
see
.BR displays .
EDID data that breaks the standard is refused unless
.B \-\-ignore\-errors
is given.
.PP
Options may come before or after the command and its arguments.
.SH READING
.TP
.BI info " file"
An overview of the display: name, manufacturer, date, input, screen size, timings,
refresh ranges, color and HDR support, audio and extension blocks.
.TP
.BI report " file"
Every group, field, value and unit, followed by the raw data.
.TP
.BI groups " file"
Every group with its offset and code, block by block; sub-groups are indented under the
group that holds them.
These are the addresses the other commands take.
.TP
.BI fields " file group"
The fields of a group with their values, units and notes: read-only, reserved, not used,
or with named values.
A detailed timing also lists its refresh rate.
.TP
.BI describe " file group field"
A field in full: value, unit, range, the bytes it occupies, its description and its
named values.
.TP
.BI get " file group field"
The value of a field, as the editor shows it.
.TP
.BI diff " file1 file2"
The fields whose values differ, by block and group, and the groups only one of the files
has.
Groups are paired by their code in order; checksums are left out.
.TP
.B displays
The connected displays whose EDID is available, with the file it is read from.
.SH CHANGING
A changed EDID is written to the file given with
.BR \-o ,
or back to the file it was read from with
.BR \-\-in\-place .
It is written as hexadecimal text when the name ends in
.I .hex
or
.IR .txt ,
or is
.B \-
for standard output, and as binary otherwise.
The new file replaces the old one only once it is complete, the checksums are recomputed,
and nothing is ever written under
.IR /sys .
What each command changed is printed, to standard error when the EDID goes to standard
output.
.TP
.BI set " file group field" = value " \fR...\fP"
Writes fields of a group, in the order given; a value a field refuses stops the command
before anything is written.
A change that alters the type or layout of a group, such as a data block tag, rebuilds
the group from its data.
Fields derived from other data, such as lengths and offsets, need
.BR \-\-edit\-read\-only .
.TP
.BI add " file block kind"
Adds a group to a block, where the editor would put it.
.I kind
is
.BR audio\-lpcm ,
.BR audio\-extended ,
.B video
or
.B timing
for a CTA-861 block, and
.B displayid
for a DisplayID block.
.TP
.BI duplicate " file group"
Copies a group after itself.
.TP
.BI delete " file group"
Removes a group.
.TP
.BI move " file group " up | down
Moves a group within its block.
.TP
.BI fix\-checksums " file"
Recomputes the checksum of every block.
.TP
.BI convert " file"
Writes the same bytes as binary or hexadecimal text.
.PP
.B fix\-checksums
and
.B convert
work on the bytes alone, so they also serve data the parser refuses.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
.TP
.BI complete " \fR[\fPword\fR...]\fP current"
Prints the candidates for the word
.I current
after the words before it, one to a line, each optionally followed by a tab and a
description.
Groups, fields and values are read from the file named in the words.
The words are the command line after the program name, without options.
Exit status 3 means that
.I current
is a file name, which the shell completes itself.
.PP
The completions for
.BR bash (1),
.BR zsh (1)
and
.BR fish (1)
installed with the program use this command.
.SH ADDRESSES
A
.I group
is named by its offset as
.B groups
lists it
.RB ( 0x036 ),
by its code when only one group has it
.RB ( MRL ),
by the nth group with a code
.RB ( DTD:2 ),
or by both
.RB ( DTD@0x036 ).
Offsets change when groups are added, moved or removed.
.PP
A
.I field
is named by the name
.B fields
shows, or by the name the parser gives it, which
.B describe
adds when it differs.
Names are matched without regard to case, spaces,
.B \-
and
.BR _ ,
so
.BR "\(dqHorizontal active\(dq" ,
.B horizontal\-active
and
.B hactivepix
are the same field.
.IB name : n
is the nth field of a name that repeats, and
.BI # n
the nth field of the group.
.PP
A detailed timing also has a
.B refresh
field, in Hz, which follows from the pixel clock and the totals.
Setting it sets the pixel clock that comes closest to the rate with the current
blanking, in steps of the clock's unit; the rate reached is printed.
A field of that name in another group, as standard timings have, is that field.
.PP
A
.I value
is text as the field shows it, a named value as
.B describe
lists it, or
.BR on ,
.BR off ,
.BR yes ,
.BR no ,
.B true
or
.B false
for a single bit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIfile\fP"
Where a changed EDID is written.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-in\-place
Writes a changed EDID back to the file it was read from.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-errors
Opens EDID data that breaks the standard, as Open Anyway does in the editor.
.TP
.B \-\-edit\-read\-only
Allows writing fields derived from other data.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Leaves out notices about the data.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Prints
.BR info ,
.BR groups ,
.BR fields ,
.B diff
and
.B displays
as JSON: an array of objects, or for
.B fields
an object holding the group and an array of its fields.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Shows a summary of the commands and options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Shows the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The data can't be read or written, a value is refused, or
.B diff
found differences.
.TP
.B 2
A mistake in the command line.
.TP
.B 3
.B complete
asks for file names.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read the EDID of a connected display:
.PP
.RS
.EX
edid\-editor\-cli displays
edid\-editor\-cli info /sys/class/drm/card1\-DP\-1/edid
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save it, raise the refresh rate of its first detailed timing, and compare:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cp /sys/class/drm/card1\-DP\-1/edid monitor.bin
edid\-editor\-cli set monitor.bin DTD:1 refresh=75 \-o new.bin
edid\-editor\-cli diff monitor.bin new.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Change fields in place, add an audio block to the first extension, and print the groups
as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.EX
edid\-editor\-cli set new.bin VID "color\-depth=10 bits" \-\-in\-place
edid\-editor\-cli add new.bin 1 audio\-lpcm \-\-in\-place
edid\-editor\-cli groups new.bin \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR edid\-editor (1),
.BR edid\-decode (1),
.BR xrandr (1)
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at
.UR https://github.com/sachesi/edid\-editor/issues
.UE .
